The files in upstream/ are copied verbatim from herdr
(https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr), src/detect/manifests/, at upstream
commit 2563803dca97c040beaf3dc3acdcb5a3221b4238. A daily workflow,
.github/workflows/herdr-manifests.yaml, opens a pull request when they change
upstream.

The manifests the engine embeds, claude.toml and codex.toml in this directory,
are those copies with laatmux's own changes applied: patches/<name>.patch,
when there is one, is a unified diff from upstream/<name> to <name>. apply.sh
rebuilds the manifests from upstream/ and patches/; the workflow runs it after
copying, so a change upstream is taken as it is and the local one goes on
top. To change a manifest here, edit it and run apply.sh refresh, which
remakes its patch. When upstream changes the lines a patch touches, the patch
no longer applies: the workflow says so in its pull request, and
TestManifestsPatched fails until the manifest is brought to what upstream and
the local change together mean and apply.sh refresh is run on that branch.

The rule engine in ../engine.go and ../regions.go is a Go port of herdr's
src/detect/manifest.rs.

